"LEADERSHIP ACADEMY FOR LOCAL GOVERNMENT (https://rm.coe.int/civil-participation-in-decision-making-toolkit-/168075c1a5)\nciteturn57view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 49; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn55view3\",\"id\":8}); Total lines: 1415\nL0@P0: CENTRE OF EXPERTISE\nL1@P0: FOR GOOD GOVERNANCE\nL2@P0: CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN\nL3@P0: DECISION- MAKING\nL4@P0: TOOLKIT\nL5@P0: Council of Europe\nL6@P0: Centre of Expertise for\nL7@P0: Good Governance\nL8@P0: The toolkit aims to provide an April 2020\nL9@P0: integrated framework that\nL10@P0: guides local authorities and\nL11@P0: practitioners, through a step\u0002by-step approach, in the\nL12@P0: design and implementation of\nL13@P0: context-based strategies to\nL14@P0: boost civil participation.\nL15@P0-1: TOOLKITCIVIL PARTICIPATION IN\nL16@P1: DECISION-MAKING\nL17@P1: Toolkit\nL18@P1: Authors\nL19@P1: CENTRE OF EXPERTISE\nL20@P1: FOR GOOD GOVERNANCE\nL21@P1: in cooperation with\nL22@P1: I S I G - Institute of International Sociology of Gorizia\nL23@P1-3: Council of EuropeThe opinions expressed in this work are the\nL24@P3: responsibility of the authors and do not necessarily\nL25@P3: reflect the official policy of the Council of Europe.\nL26@P3: All rights reserved. No part of this publication may\nL27@P3: be translated, reproduced or transmitted, in any\nL28@P3: form or by any means, electronic (CD-Rom,\nL29@P3: internet, etc.) or mechanical, including\nL30@P3: photocopying, recording or any information\nL31@P3: storage or retrieval system, without prior\nL32@P3: permission in writing from the Centre of Expertise\nL33@P3: for Local Government Reform.\nL34@P3: Council of Europe\nL35@P3: Centre of Expertise for Good Governance\nL36@P3: https://www.coe.int/en/web/good\u0002governance/centre-of-expertise\nL37@P3: © Centre of Expertise for Good Governance,\nL38@P3-5: 2017, 2020CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN DECISION-MAKING ●\nL39@P5: CENTRE OF EXPERTISE FOR GOOD GOVERNANCE\nL40@P5: Since its inception, the Centre of Expertise has operated in a rapidly evolving context of\nL41@P5: decentralisation and governance: the transfer of functions from the national to the local and/or\nL42@P5: regional levels has advanced in most member states, and decentralisation policies have captured\nL43@P5: increasing attention. The tools and methods of the Centre of Expertise have developed over the\nL44@P5: course of its mandate to reflect these changes and emerging issues.\nL45@P5: In this context, the Centre’s capacity-building programmes and legal assistance activities support the\nL46@P5: on-going process of reform of local government but are increasingly aimed not only at local but also\nL47@P5: at regional and central authorities. The Centre is now in a position to offer cutting-edge expertise on\nL48@P5: multi-level governance to its partners and beneficiaries.\nL49@P5: The practical and impact-oriented “specific projects” are implemented in cooperation with local,\nL50@P5: regional, national and international stakeholders and are aimed both at improving the legislation and\nL51@P5: at strengthening the institutional capacity of all tiers of government.\nL52@P5: The Centre is uniquely placed to balance the needs of central and local authorities to support multi\u0002level governance. While maintaining an approach that focuses on understanding the needs of local\nL53@P5: governance actors, the Centre’s connection to the Council of Europe’s intergovernmental Committee\nL54@P5: on Democracy and Governance (CDDG) offers it ready access to high-level government officials from\nL55@P5: the 47 member states with a reservoir of knowledge and expertise in governance reforms.\nL56@P5: Today, the Centre of Expertise aims to promote the relevant European standards such as the\nL57@P5: European Charter for Local Self-Government, and the 12 Principles of Good Governance through\nL58@P5: legal and policy advice, and through implementation of benchmarks, evaluation instruments and\nL59@P5: innovative methodologies (“tools”).\nL60@P5: ISIG – Institute of International Sociology Gorizia\nL61@P5: ISIG is an independent research institute in the field of social sciences.\nL62@P5: Founded in 1968 in Gorizia (Italy), ISIG envisages a future of peaceful relations fostered by an\nL63@P5: international understanding, based on the acknowledgment of differences as resources.\nL64@P5: ISIG carries out research at national and international levels, gathering knowledge on the problems\nL65@P5: arising from relations between states, ethnic groups and on the cultural, economic and social\nL66@P5: development of communities.\nL67@P5: Participatory processes and participated local development processes are a key feature of ISIG work.\nL68@P5: Its research activities focused on the development of research methods and interpretative models\nL69@P5: that allow the development of innovative strategies and the effective implementation of policies at\nL70@P5: local level. Within this perspective, ISIG ultimate goal is to facilitate the inclusion of local\nL71@P5: stakeholders in the policy-making processes.\nL72@P5: Through a wide range of participatory approaches and methodologies, ISIG achieves the integration\nL73@P5: of stakeholders, starting with an analysis and comparison of mediated social variables (i.e. the\nL74@P5: integrative function of the community, the role of symbolic spaces, the projection into the future and\nL75@P5: the actions needed to achieve it). This ultimately enables the integrated development of the local\nL76@P5: context, analysing the factors (internal and external) that may favour or not policy implementation,\nL77@P5-6: allowing for ex-ante assessment and project feasibility testing.4 ┇\nL78@P6: ● Toolkit\nL79@P6: INDEX\nL80@P6: 1 INTRODUCTION ....................................................................................................................................... 6\nL81@P6: 1.1 BACKGROUND...........