........................................................................................... 20\nL107@P6: 3.3.6 Economic capital dimension............................................................................................................ 20\nL108@P6: 3.3.7 Human capital dimension................................................................................................................ 21\nL109@P6: 3.3.8 Political capital dimension............................................................................................................... 21\nL110@P6: 3.4 STAKEHOLDERS’ IDENTIFICATION AND EVALUATION......................................................................................... 22\nL111@P6: 3.4.1 Stakeholders’ identification............................................................................................................. 22\nL112@P6: 3.4.2 Stakeholder’s Evaluation................................................................................................................. 23\nL113@P6: 4 THE STRATEGIES .................................................................................................................................... 32\nL114@P6: 4.1 PARTICIPATION IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGIES ............................................................................................... 32\nL115@P6: 4.1.1 Quadrant 1 – Information ............................................................................................................... 32\nL116@P6: 4.1.2 Quadrant 2 – Consultation .............................................................................................................. 33\nL117@P6: 4.1.3 Quadrant 3 – Dialogue .................................................................................................................... 36\nL118@P6: 4.1.4 Quadrant 4 – Partnership................................................................................................................ 38\nL119@P6: 4.2 PARTICIPATION ENHANCEMENT STRATEGIES ................................................................................................... 39\nL120@P6: 4.2.1 Strategy 1 – From Information to Consultation .............................................................................. 40\nL121@P6: 4.2.2 Strategy 2 – From Consultation to Dialogue ................................................................................... 41\nL122@P6: 4.2.3 Strategy 3 – From Dialogue to Partnership ..................................................................................... 41\nL123@P6: 4.2.4 Strategy 4 – Strengthening of Partnership...................................................................................... 42\nL124@P6-7: 5 REFERENCES........................................................................................................................................... 43Index ┇ 5\nL125@P7: CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN DECISION-MAKING ●\nL126@P7: LIST OF TABLES\nL127@P7: Table 1 - Evaluation grid for social capital within the \"Community Evaluation\" .................................. 20\nL128@P7: Table 2 - Evaluation grid for economic capital within the \"Community Evaluation\"............................ 21\nL129@P7: Table 3 - Evaluation grid for human capital within the \"Community Evaluation\" ................................ 21\nL130@P7: Table 4 - Evaluation grid for political capital within the \"Community Evaluation\"............................... 21\nL131@P7: Table 5 - Example of systematisation of the stakeholders' database................................................... 23\nL132@P7: Table 6 - Social capital variables within evaluation grid for relevance ................................................. 25\nL133@P7: Table 7 - Economic capital variables within evaluation grid for relevance........................................... 25\nL134@P7: Table 8 - Human capital variables within evaluation grid for relevance............................................... 26\nL135@P7: Table 9 - Political capital variables within evaluation grid for relevance.............................................. 26\nL136@P7: Table 10 - Social capital variables within evaluation grid for interest .................................................. 28\nL137@P7: Table 11 - Economic capital variables within evaluation grid for interest............................................ 28\nL138@P7: Table 12 - Human capital variables within evaluation grid for interest................................................ 29\nL139@P7: Table 13 - Political capital variables within evaluation grid for interest............................................... 29\nL140@P7: Table 14 - Typologies and levels of stakeholders’ involvement............................................................ 31\nL141@P7: LIST OF FIGURES\nL142@P7: Figure 1 - Enforcing trust for mainstreaming participation .................................................................... 6\nL143@P7: Figure 2 - Objectives of the Toolkit ......................................................................................................... 7\nL144@P7: Figure 3 - Theoretical pillars of the Toolkit ............................................................................................. 9\nL145@P7: Figure 4 - Key concepts for understanding civil participation............................................................... 10\nL146@P7: Figure 5 - C.L.E.A.R. key principles ........................................................................................................ 12\nL147@P7: Figure 6 - Citizens’ engagement Paradigms.......................................................................................... 13\nL148@P7: Figure 7 - Structure of the Tool ............................................................................................................. 15\nL149@P7: Figure 8 - The dimensions relevant for participation............................................................................ 16\nL150@P7: Figure 9 - Overview of the \"Community Evaluation\" page of the tool ................................................. 20\nL151@P7: Figure 10 - Summary table for the \"Community Evaluation\" scores per dimension ............................ 22\nL152@P7: Figure 11 - Overview of the Relevance section of the \"Stakeholders Identification and Evaluation\"\nL153@P7: page of the tool ..................................................................................................................................... 24\nL154@P7: Figure 12 - Overview of the interest section of the \"Stakeholder Identification and Evaluation\" page\nL155@P7: of the tool.............................................................................................................................................. 27\nL156@P7: Figure 13 - Stakeholder plotting in the Tool.......................................................................................... 29\nL157@P7: Figure 14 - Taxonomy for stakeholders’ identification ......................................................................... 30\nL158@P7: Figure 15 - Strategies for increasing the level of participation of stakeholders................................... 40\nL159@P7: ACRONYMS\nL160@P7: CBC – Cross-Border Cooperation\nL161@P7: CoE – Council of Europe\nL162@P7: CSO – Civil Society Organisation\nL163@P7: LA – Local Authority\nL164@P7-8: NGO – Non-Governmental Organisation6 ┇\nL165@P8: ● Toolkit\nL166@P8: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL167@P8: 1.1 BACKGROUND\nL168@P8: “Participation of citizens is at the very heart of the idea of democracy.”\nL169@P8: 1 Effective democracy\nL170@P8: depends on citizens having a say and being heard. A commitment to enhanced public participation\nL171@P8: lies at the heart of the 2018 Council of Europe (CoE) Recommendation on the participation of citizens\nL172@P8: in local public life. The aim is to see consultation and participation embedded in the culture of all CoE\nL173@P8: member states.\nL174@P8: Participation by all groups of civil society in\nL175@P8: decision-making at all levels of government is one\nL176@P8: of the prerequisites for a functioning democratic\nL177@P8: society and for fostering democratic security. It\nL178@P8: allows for open dialogue on critical issues,\nL179@P8: supporting better decisions by the authorities and\nL180@P8: contributing to strengthen governance. Civil\nL181@P8: participation complements and supports\nL182@P8: representative democracy. Citizens who feel that\nL183@P8: they have a say in the general policy debate and in\nL184@P8: everyday decisions, are more likely to accept the\nL185@P8: decisions taken and, more generally, to trust their\nL186@P8: elected representatives. It is therefore crucial that\nL187@P8: individuals, non-governmental organisations\nL188@P8: (NGOs) and civil society at large are involved in the\nL189@P8: conduct of public affairs and feel empowered to do\nL190@P8: so2.\nL191@P8: Deploying effective civil participation mechanisms\nL192@P8: to deliver good local governance means that\nL193@P8: stakeholders’ engagement should be fostered\nL194@P8: inclusively and transparently. Effective participation\nL195@P8: also implies to move beyond the assumption that\nL196@P8: all can participate in all circumstances and in all\nL197@P8: phases of decision-making. Opening decisional\nL198@P8: processes to all would mean having to respond to\nL199@P8: all and integrate each statement and insight in the\nL200@P8: development of the strategy or policy at hand, to\nL201@P8: respect the mutual pact of trust established in the\nL202@P8: moment the participatory process was launched. To make this feasible (i.e. being able to integrate\nL203@P8: stakeholders’ perspective in the design of the new policy/strategy), effective participation implies a\nL204@P8: careful planning of the stakeholders to be involved and the level of involvement for each, for each\nL205@P8: stage of the decision-making process on a given topic.\nL206@P8: \nL207@P8: 1 Council of Europe, CM/Rec(2018)4\nL208@P8: 2 Council of Europe, CM(2017)83, Guidelines for civil participation in political decision making.\nL209@P8: Sustainable\nL210@P8: participation\nL211@P8: Mapping\nL212@P8: stakeholders\nL213@P8: Understanding\nL214@P8: stakeholders\nL215@P8: involvement\nL216@P8: Guaranteeing\nL217@P8: respect\nL218@P8: Enforcing\nL219@P8: mutual trust\nL220@P8-9: Figure 1 - Enforcing trust for mainstreaming participationIntroduction ┇ 7\nL221@P9: CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN DECISION-MAKING ●\nL222@P9: The CoE efforts in the field of promotion and strengthening of civil participation across Member\nL223@P9: states may be drawn from the following documents:\nL224@P9:  Charter on Local Self-government (CETS 122)\nL225@P9:  Convention on the Participation of foreigners in public life at local level (CETS 144)\nL226@P9:  Recommendation (2001) on participation (replaced by Rec (2018)4)\nL227@P9:  Recommendation (2009) on evaluating, auditing and monitoring participation and\nL228@P9: participation policies at local and regional level\nL229@P9:  CoE C.L.E.A.R. tool on civil participation (2008)\nL230@P9:  CoE Toolkit for increasing Civil Participation in Cross-Border Governance systems –\nL231@P9: “European Experience of Citizens' Participation in Cross-Border Governance” (2015)\nL232@P9:  CoE Guidelines for Civil Participation (2017)\nL233@P9:  Recommendation CM/Rec (2018)4 the Committee of Ministers to member States on the\nL234@P9: participation of citizens in local public life\nL235@P9: This Toolkit aims to channel all these insights and recommendations into an integrated framework\nL236@P9: that guides local authorities and practitioners, through a step-by-step approach, in the design and\nL237@P9: implementation of context-based strategies to increase a community’s civil participation.\nL238@P9: The toolkit aims:\nL239@P9: Figure 2 - Objectives of the Toolkit\nL240@P9: 1.2 TOOLKIT STRUCTURE\nL241@P9: The Toolkit is structured in three sections:\nL242@P9:  a compendium of the main CoE frameworks that set the principles of civil participation;\nL243@P9:  operational indications on how to use the tool to understand the propensity towards\nL244@P9: participation of the community at large, as well as to analyse in detail the potential of each\nL245@P9: stakeholder to engage in participatory process on a given topic;\nL246@P9:  an overview of strategies to activate the processes of participation for different\nL247@P9: stakeholders, based on the assessment of their potential to engage, and to enhance the level\nL248@P9: of participation.\nL249@P9: •To contextualise the role of civil participation for efficient and effective local\nL250@P9: governance, as defined by Council of Europe standards and frameworks\nL251@P9: Role of civil participation in local governance\nL252@P9: •To support LAs in understanding their current community context and the potential\nL253@P9: of actual engagement of each stakeholder in the decision-making process\nL254@P9: Understanding the level of participation in local contexts\nL255@P9: •To guide LAs in the choice of dedicated strategies and actions for different levels of\nL256@P9: involvement, at different stages of the decision-making process.\nL257@P9-11: Support the adoption or strategies for enhancing participationFramework of civil participation ┇ 9\nL258@P11: CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN DECISION-MAKING ●\nL259@P11: 2 THE FRAMEWORK OF CIVIL PARTICIPATION\nL260@P11: 2.1 RATIONALE\nL261@P11: This section aims to give a brief overview of the Council of Europe’s guidelines and frameworks on\nL262@P11: civil participation in political decision-making.\nL263@P11: The present work stems from the frameworks and principles of civil participation, set by the Council\nL264@P11: of Europe over the last years.\nL265@P11: The main pillars that stand at the basis of this Toolkit are:\nL266@P11: Figure 3 - Theoretical pillars of the Toolkit\nL267@P11: Furthermore, the Toolkit reflects general principles and standards for participation set out in the\nL268@P11: Revised Code of Good Practice for Civil participation3. The Code, first adopted by the Conference of\nL269@P11: INGO in 2009 (CONF/PLE(2009)CODE1) and revised in 2019, sets a framework for the facilitation of a\nL270@P11: structured dialogue and cooperation between decision makers and civil society. Moreover, the\nL271@P11: Toolkit shares its vision of a multi-stakeholder and multiple levels approach to civil participation.\nL272@P11: Aiming to contribute to the harmonisation of the CoE continuously evolving corpus of knowledge and\nL273@P11: tools facilitating civil participation, the Toolkit is envisaged as a step forward towards an effective\nL274@P11: implementation of the principles of civil participation.\nL275@P11: 2.2 KEY CONCEPTS4\nL276@P11: The following paragraphs provide some useful definitions of key-concepts that recur in the toolkit.\nL277@P11: \nL278@P11: 3\nL279@P11:  Conference of INGOs of the Council of Europe, Code of Good practice for Civil participation – 2019.\nL280@P11: 4 Definitions of key concepts from: Council of Europe, CDLR. (2008): C.L.E.A.R. Tool, p.45-47.\nL281@P11: 2008\nL282@P11: •C.L.E.A.R. Tool\nL283@P11: 2015\nL284@P11: •European Experience of\nL285@P11: Citizens' Participation in\nL286@P11: Cross-Border Governance\nL287@P11: 2017\nL288@P11: •Guidelines for Civil\nL289@P11: Participation\nL290@P11: 2018\nL291@P11: •Recommendation on the\nL292@P11: participation of citizens in\nL293@P11: local public life (replacing\nL294@P11-12: the 2001 Rec)10 ┇\nL295@P12: ● Toolkit\nL296@P12: Figure 4 - Key concepts for understanding civil participation\nL297@P12: 2.2.1 Community\nL298@P12: A ‘community’ can be defined as a group of interacting people living in a common location.\nL299@P12: Community is sometimes defined as a tighter and more cohesive social entity compared to ‘society’,\nL300@P12: due to the presence within a community of a ‘unity of will’. Communitarism construes communities\nL301@P12: as originating from the voluntary acts of pre-community individuals. It emphasizes the role of the\nL302@P12: community in defining and shaping individuals and their identity. From a communitarian perspective,\nL303@P12: values and beliefs cannot exist outside the public space, in which debate takes place. This suggests\nL304@P12: that community is a condition and a result of participation. It creates a ‘sense of community’, which\nL305@P12: from a psychological perspective needs:\nL306@P12:  membership,\nL307@P12:  influence,\nL308@P12:  integration and fulfilment of needs, and\nL309@P12:  shared emotional connection.\nL310@P12: 2.2.2 Civil society\nL311@P12: Civil society is composed of the totality of voluntary civic and social organisations and institutions\nL312@P12: that form the basis of a functioning society as opposed to the force-backed structures of a state\nL313@P12: (regardless of that state's political system) and commercial institutions.\nL314@P12: 2.2.3 Civic organisations\nL315@P12: Civic organisations are structures in which civil society is organised. It comprises NGOs such as\nL316@P12: groups, associations, movements. It is defined here in contrast to governmental organisations.\nL317@P12: 2.2.4 Civic activity\nL318@P12: Civic activity is the outcome of the work of civic organisations. In a restricted sense, these activities\nL319@P12: would need to be in the service of the community. Even though the term does not include individual,\nL320@P12: privately orientated activity, it seems difficult to convincingly differentiate them in terms of purpose.\nL321@P12: 2.2.5 Social capital\nL322@P12: Social capital refers to the collective value of all ‘social networks’ (i.e. who people know) and the\nL323@P12: inclinations that arise from these networks to do things for each other (i.e. norms of reciprocity). It is\nL324@P12: the stocks of social trust, norms and networks that people can draw on to solve common problems.\nL325@P12: Community\nL326@P12: Civil Society\nL327@P12: Civic\nL328@P12: Organisations\nL329@P12: Social Capital Civic Activity\nL330@P12: Decision-making\nL331@P12: process\nL332@P12-13: Civil SocietyFramework of civil participation ┇ 11\nL333@P13: CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN DECISION-MAKING ●\nL334@P13: 2.2.6 Decision making process\nL335@P13: Decision making process refers to the development, adoption, implementation, evaluation and\nL336@P13: reformulation of a policy document, strategy, law, regulation, or any process where a decision is\nL337@P13: made that affects the public or a segment of it by the authority invested with the power to do so.\nL338@P13: 2.2.7 Civil society at large\nL339@P13: Civil society at large refers to the ensemble of individuals organised, less organised and informal\nL340@P13: groups through which they contribute to society or express their views and opinions, including NGOs,\nL341@P13: professional and grass-roots organisations, universities and research centres, religious and non\u0002denominational organisations, human rights defenders, watchdogs and whistle-blowers.\nL342@P13: 2.3 CIVIL PARTICIPATION\nL343@P13: “Civil participation means the engagement of individuals, NGOs and civil society at large in decision\u0002making processes by public authorities”5\nL344@P13: . Civil participation in political decision-making should seek\nL345@P13: to provide, collect and channel views of individuals, directly or via civil society organisations (CSOs),\nL346@P13: providing a substantive exchange of factual and evidence-based information and views that inform\nL347@P13: the decision-making process and ensure that real public needs are met6.\nL348@P13: Yet, getting people to participate is not a simple task. There are obstacles that often stem from a lack\nL349@P13: of capacity to participate or a lack of engagement with political organisations or issues.\nL350@P13: This might be caused by the fact that citizens, including politicians, are not always well informed\nL351@P13: about the implication of participation and the role they can play in the democratic life of the\nL352@P13: community. Without appropriate knowledge, civil society cannot actively integrate a participatory\nL353@P13: approach in their daily discourses, consequently, in local identity.\nL354@P13: 2.3.1 Recommendation on the participation of citizens in local public life\nL355@P13: The first Recommendation on participation was adopted by the Committee of Ministers of the\nL356@P13: Council of Europe in 2001, and in 2018 it was replaced by an updated version. The Recommendation\nL357@P13: identifies a set of basic principles of a local democratic policy and the related steps and measures to\nL358@P13: encourage and reinforce citizens’ participation in local public life.7It aims to serve as a helpful guide\nL359@P13: for local and national authorities committed to improving the opportunities for citizen participation\nL360@P13: in the life of the local community. It contains five recommendations to Council of Europe member\nL361@P13: states’ governments on: framing a policy; adopting measures to improve the legal framework for\nL362@P13: participation and ensuring that national legislation and regulations enable local and regional\nL363@P13: authorities to employ a wide range of participation instruments; co-operating with authorities on\nL364@P13: different levels; reviewing policies related to the participation of citizens in local public life and\nL365@P13: ensuring the translation of the recommendation into national languages.\nL366@P13: The appendix to the Recommendation is divided into two sections: Section A sets out the basic\nL367@P13: general principles of a local democratic participation policy. These should guide policymakers at\nL368@P13: national level in the drafting of a framework for participation and should also be subscribed to by\nL369@P13: local and regional authorities; Section B outlines steps and measures that could be taken by the\nL370@P13: competent public authorities.\nL371@P13: 5 Council of Europe, CM(2017)83, Guidelines for civil participation in political decision making, p.2\nL372@P13: 6 Council of Europe, CM(2017)83, Guidelines for civil participation in political decision making, p.3\nL373@P13-14: 7 Council of Europe, CM/Rec(2018)4.12 ┇\nL374@P14: ● Toolkit\nL375@P14: 2.3.2 C.L.E.A.R.: An auditing tool for citizen participation at the local level\nL376@P14: In 2008, the Council of Europe published the C.L.E.A.R. tool8, in order to aid public authorities in\nL377@P14: understanding their communities’ propensity and capacity to participate, as well as their own\nL378@P14: resources to support this process.\nL379@P14: The main principles on which C.L.E.A.R. is based\nL380@P14: are described in the next paragraphs.\nL381@P14: 2.3.2.1 Legitimisation and Accountability\nL382@P14: Local authorities are not able to act as effective\nL383@P14: community leaders if they lack a base of popular\nL384@P14: support. More generally, there is a need to\nL385@P14: strengthen public confidence in political\nL386@P14: institutions and the most powerful way to do so\nL387@P14: is to seek active citizen endorsement of policies\nL388@P14: and practices. Deliberative elements of\nL389@P14: democracy give citizens a voice, creating\nL390@P14: indispensable long-term loyalty to the political\nL391@P14: system. It is not necessary for citizens’ individual\nL392@P14: voices to be decisive. It is part of democracy\nL393@P14: that the majority decides. But what counts is\nL394@P14: that one’s voice has been considered.\nL395@P14: 2.3.2.2 Learning to respond to citizens’ needs\nL396@P14: Effective channels of communication are essential\nL397@P14: to achieving the wider social and economic\nL398@P14: outcomes that Local authorities seek to achieve. Participation enables more effective learning and\nL399@P14: better decisions.\nL400@P14: 2.3.2.3 Fostering sense of ownership of local institutions\nL401@P14: Participation has an intrinsic value. It is good that people are actively involved in decision making in\nL402@P14: their communities. Being a full citizen means having a say in decisions that one is affected by. Good\nL403@P14: governance is not just a matter of delivering good outcomes. The way they are achieved is at least as\nL404@P14: important. Public authorities at all levels should seek citizens’ active endorsement of rather than tacit\nL405@P14: acquiescence to their policies and programmes.\nL406@P14: Aiming to make these general principles accessible for all public authorities, the Council of Europe\nL407@P14: developed a set of key questions to function as a preliminary self-assessment. These guiding\nL408@P14: questions relate to the 5 dimensions of analysis that the acronym ‘C.L.E.A.R.’ embodies:\nL409@P14:  Can do – that is, citizens have the resources and skills and knowledge to participate;\nL410@P14:  Like to – that is, citizens have a sense of attachment that reinforces participation;\nL411@P14:  Enabled to – that is, citizens are provided with the opportunity to participate;\nL412@P14:  Asked to – that is, citizens are involved by official bodies or voluntary groups;\nL413@P14:  Responded to – that is, citizens see evidence that their views have been considered.\nL414@P14: \nL415@P14: 8 Council of Europe, CDLR. (2008). C.L.E.A.R. Tool C.L.E.A.R.\nL416@P14: Legitimisation and Accountability\nL417@P14: Learning to respond to citizens’\nL418@P14: needs\nL419@P14: Fostering sense of ownership of\nL420@P14: local institutions\nL421@P14-15: Figure 5 - C.L.E.A.R. key principlesFramework of civil participation ┇ 13\nL422@P15: CIVIL PARTICIPATION IN DECISION-MAKING ●\nL423@P15: C.L.E.A.R. functions through a set of key-questions for each of the above-mentioned sections. By\nL424@P15: answering to these questions, public authorities can appraise their own and their communities’\nL425@P15: strengths and weaknesses in relation to the capacity to start and develop participatory processes,\n"
